{"t": 15.894, "kind": "hook", "index": 5, "sleep_procs": ["61273 61263 SN         00:09 sleep 601"], "payload": {"session_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000000", "transcript_path": "/Users/uuuuu/orca-qa/r1r2-capture/home/.claude/projects/-Users-uuuuu-orca-qa-r1r2-capture-work/00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000000.jsonl", "cwd": "/Users/uuuuu/orca-qa/r1r2-capture/work", "prompt_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000001", "hook_event_name": "MessageDisplay", "turn_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000002", "message_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000003", "index": 0, "final": false, "delta": "The `sleep 601` command is running in the background, and I haven't checked on it. Here's the essay:\n\n"}}
{"t": 15.975, "kind": "hook", "index": 6, "sleep_procs": ["61273 61263 SN         00:09 sleep 601"], "payload": {"session_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000000", "transcript_path": "/Users/uuuuu/orca-qa/r1r2-capture/home/.claude/projects/-Users-uuuuu-orca-qa-r1r2-capture-work/00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000000.jsonl", "cwd": "/Users/uuuuu/orca-qa/r1r2-capture/work", "prompt_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000001", "hook_event_name": "MessageDisplay", "turn_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000002", "message_id": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-0000b1000003", "index": 1, "final": false, "delta": "---\n\n# The Keepers of the Edge: On Lighthouses\n\nA lighthouse makes a promise in the dark. It stands where land gives way to sea and tells every passing ship the same thing: *here is danger, and here is where you are.* For more than two thousand years people have built these towers on headlands, reefs and barren rocks. They are among the most practical buildings ever made, and also among the most romantic. Few structures have gathered so much meaning while doing such a simple job.\n\nThe story usually starts with the Pharos of Alexandria, built around 280 BCE under Ptolemy II. Ancient writers said it stood more than a hundred meters tall, and it counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. A fire burned at the top, and some accounts say a polished bronze mirror threw its light far out to sea. The Pharos was so famous that its name became the word for lighthouse in several languages: *phare* in French, *faro* in Italian and Spanish. Earthquakes finally brought it down in the fourteenth century, but it had set the pattern for everything after it. Build high, burn bright, and be seen.\n\nFor centuries after that, lighthouses changed very little. Keepers burned wood, coal and later candles or oil lamps, and the light rarely carried far. Many towers were little more than fire baskets on poles. The Industrial Revolution changed this, as it changed so much else. Maritime trade grew quickly, and so did shipwrecks. Governments began to treat coastal lighting as a public duty and not a private favor. Engineers such as John Smeaton, who built the third Eddystone Lighthouse off Plymouth in the 1750s, worked out how to put a structure on wave-battered rock and keep it standing. Smeaton fitted his granite blocks together with dovetail joints and shaped the tower like an oak trunk, wide at the base and narrowing as it rose. That approach shaped lighthouse building for generations.\n\n"}}
